          "is the battery, where that noble mole \nis washed by waves, and cooled by \nbreezes, which a few hours previous \nwere out of sight of land. Look at the \ncrowds of water-gazers there.\nCircumambulate the city of a \ndreamy Sabbath afternoon. Go from \nCorlears Hook to Coenties Slip, and \nfrom thence, by Whitehall, northward. \nWhat do you see?—Posted like silent \nsentinels all around the town, stand \nthousands upon thousands of mortal \nmen fixed in ocean reveries. Some \nleaning against the spiles; some \nseated upon the pier-heads; some \nlooking over the bulwarks of ships \nfrom China; some high aloft in the \nrigging, as if striving to get a still \nbetter seaward peep. But these are \nall landsmen; of week days pent up in \nlath and plaster—tied to counters, \nnailed to benches, clinched to desks. "
